`try_join_all` can drop a shard worker-restart future after workers are stopped, leaving the local shard update handler stopped

## Root Cause

`Collection::recreate_optimizers` runs per-shard optimizer worker restarts concurrently with `future::try_join_all`. This is a fail-fast combinator: when one shard future returns `Err`, unfinished sibling futures are dropped.

That matters here because the per-shard restart path is explicitly documented as not cancel safe, and its local-shard implementation has a real partial-progress state:

```text
stop update/optimizer/flush workers
    -> wait for workers and take the pending update receiver
    -> await again before restart
    -> run_workers(update_receiver)
```

If one shard returns an error while another shard is suspended after `wait_workers_stops()` but before `run_workers(update_receiver)`, `try_join_all` can drop the suspended sibling future. That sibling has already stopped its update pipeline, but it never gets to restart it.

Analyzed revision: `44ad62f8cd69642be5afa6441612525e24a0d063`.

The collection-level fail-fast composition is here:

```rust
// lib/collection/src/collection/collection_ops.rs
async fn recreate_optimizers(shards_holder: SharedShardHolder) -> CollectionResult<()> {
    let shard_holder = shards_holder.read().await;
    let updates = shard_holder
        .all_shards()
        .map(|replica_set| replica_set.on_optimizer_config_update());
    future::try_join_all(updates).await?;
    Ok(())
}
```

The called future is documented as not cancel safe at the replica-set layer:

```rust
// lib/collection/src/shards/replica_set/mod.rs
/// ## Cancel safety
///
/// This function is **not** cancel safe.
pub(crate) async fn on_optimizer_config_update(&self) -> CollectionResult<()> {
    let read_local = self.local.read().await;
    if let Some(shard) = &*read_local {
        shard.on_optimizer_config_update().await
    } else {
        Ok(())
    }
}
```

The shard enum delegation is also documented as not cancel safe:

```rust
// lib/collection/src/shards/shard.rs
/// ## Cancel safety
///
/// This function is **not** cancel safe.
pub async fn on_optimizer_config_update(&self) -> CollectionResult<()> {
    match self {
        Shard::Local(local_shard) => local_shard.on_optimizer_config_update().await,
        Shard::Proxy(proxy_shard) => proxy_shard.on_optimizer_config_update().await,
        Shard::ForwardProxy(proxy_shard) => proxy_shard.on_optimizer_config_update().await,
        Shard::QueueProxy(proxy_shard) => proxy_shard.on_optimizer_config_update().await,
        Shard::Dummy(dummy_shard) => dummy_shard.on_optimizer_config_update(),
    }
}
```

The local-shard implementation is where the bad intermediate state is created:

```rust
